You are grounding a direct weight-edit task. Before any weight of the target model is touched, establish what the model actually does under the current harness — measured, not assumed — and whether the sampling configuration itself explains the reported bug.

## Doctrine

Measure before you diagnose. In the anchor case, a Qwen reasoning model reported as "bad at math" was finishing almost no math answers at all: 85 of its first 96 practice answers ran to the token limit. The "math bug" was a completion bug. A baseline audit that counts answer classes — correct-and-complete, wrong-but-complete, non-terminating — is the ground every later step stands on; the baseline counts ARE the model's measured behavior.

The vendor model card is part of the ground. The anchor ran its target in thinking mode — the exact condition the vendor card warns is prone to repetitive loops — at a lower temperature than the card recommends. A bug that disappears at the card's recommended settings is a settings bug, not a weight bug, and no weight edit should be attempted to fix it.

